  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n6 0 6\n0 6 6\n6 0 6\n4 0 4\n0 4 4\n4 0 4\n8 8 8\n8 0 8\n8 8 8\nOutput:\n8 8 8\n8 0 8\n8 8 8\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n2 0 0 3 0 0 7 0 7 1 0 0\n2 0 0 3 0 0 0 7 0 1 0 0\n0 2 2 0 3 3 7 0 7 0 1 1\nOutput:\n7 0 7\n0 7 0\n7 0 7\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n3 0 0 4 0 4 2 0 0 8 0 0 1 0 0\n0 3 3 4 4 4 0 2 2 0 8 8 0 1 1\n0 3 0 4 0 4 0 2 0 0 8 0 0 1 0\nOutput:\n4 0 4\n4 4 4\n4 0 4\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n0 7 7\n7 7 0\n7 0 7\n3 0 0\n0 3 3\n3 0 0\n2 0 0\n0 2 2\n2 0 0\n8 0 0\n0 8 8\n8 0 0\nOutput:\n0 7 7\n7 7 0\n7 0 7\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n0 5 0\n5 0 5\n0 5 0\n0 3 0\n3 0 3\n0 3 0\n6 0 6\n6 6 0\n6 0 6\n0 4 0\n4 0 4\n0 4 0\n0 8 0\n8 0 8\n0 8 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    The input consists of several 3\u00d73 blocks arranged either vertically\n    (multiple rows of blocks) or horizontally (multiple columns of blocks).\n    Among all blocks, we select the one that contains the fewest zeros\n    (i.e., the most filled with non\u2011zero cells) and return it.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dimensions\n    h = len(grid)\n    w = len(grid[0]) if h else 0\n\n    # number of 3\u00d73 blocks along each axis\n    nrows = h // 3\n    ncols = w // 3\n\n    best_block = None\n    min_zeros = None\n\n    for bi in range(nrows):\n        for bj in range(ncols):\n            # extract the 3\u00d73 sub\u2011grid\n            block = [row[bj*3:(bj+1)*3] for row in grid[bi*3:(bi+1)*3]]\n            # count zeros inside the block\n            zeros = sum(cell == 0 for row in block for cell in row)\n            if min_zeros is None or zeros < min_zeros:\n                min_zeros = zeros\n                best_block = block\n\n    return best_block",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    The input consists of several 3\u00d73 blocks arranged either vertically\n    (multiple rows of blocks) or horizontally (multiple columns of blocks).\n    Among all blocks, we select the one that contains the fewest zeros\n    (i.e., the most filled with non\u2011zero cells) and return it.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dimensions\n    h = len(grid)\n    w = len(grid[0]) if h else 0\n\n    # number of 3\u00d73 blocks along each axis\n    nrows = h // 3\n    ncols = w // 3\n\n    best_block = None\n    min_zeros = None\n\n    for bi in range(nrows):\n        for bj in range(ncols):\n            # extract the 3\u00d73 sub\u2011grid\n            block = [row[bj*3:(bj+1)*3] for row in grid[bi*3:(bi+1)*3]]\n            # count zeros inside the block\n            zeros = sum(cell == 0 for row in block for cell in row)\n            if min_zeros is None or zeros < min_zeros:\n                min_zeros = zeros\n                best_block = block\n\n    return best_block",
